WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.420
The end of the world is a topic as old as
the world itself.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.330
As long as we have lived, we’ve realized
that all this has to come to a stop someday.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:14.720
It’s just a question of when, of how, and
of whether there’s anything we can do about

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:15.720
it.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.699
There are thousands upon thousands of different
interpretations of how exactly it will all

00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:21.180
come to an end.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.369
Some say it ends with a bang; a massive flood,
a plague of locusts, a rain of fire.

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:33.030
Some say it ends with a whimper; the quiet
death of all life, the stars winking out all

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.750
at once, the world going to sleep one day
and never waking back up.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.219
So how does it all end?

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:44.720
Well, like many great tragedies, Achilles
felled by a single arrow, a tiny tremor becoming

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:51.300
a deadly tsunami, or a tiny molecule bringing
the end for billions, it begins with something

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.360
small.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.600
Something seemingly insignificant.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.149
It begins with a Lock.

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:03.730
In 1654, a minor Scottish aristocrat named
Sir Edwin Young was traveling across the Mesopotamian

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.840
desert in search of something most unusual.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.140
He was eager to find a new item for his Cabinet
of Curiosities, a room in his home filled

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.430
with bizarre discoveries from all around the
globe.

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:21.340
Sculptures of twisted beasts, pickled creatures
gazing lifelessly from jars, and jewels plucked

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.380
from the - likely cursed - tombs of long-dead
royalty.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.920
On this particular trip, he found his favorite
item yet, which he described in his journals

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:36.320
as a “bound jewel of onyx and filigree gold,
of fineness beyond rational statement.”

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.479
It was found in an ancient ruin, thought to
be the temple of “a fearsome death god.”

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:45.900
Though Sir Young seemed to understand the
gravity of his discovery, he had no idea what

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:52.100
he had found, or that it was yet another world-altering
instance of SCP-001.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:58.869
SCP-001 is a complicated topic, one we’ve
covered on here many times before.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:03.430
Every time you think you understand exactly
what it means, there’s a new curveball coming

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.460
directly at your face.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.410
There are nightmarish monsters, a sun that’s
turned against all human life, more secret

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:14.900
origins for the Foundation and perhaps all
life than you can even count on your fingers,

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.611
a guardian with a flaming sword, a great red
monarch hoping to bring an end to all light

00:02:19.611 --> 00:02:27.540
and happiness in the multiverse, and now there’s
SCP-001 The Lock, or qntm’s proposal.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.780
After Young’s death, his descendants donated
his collection to the Scottish National Museum.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:37.390
There, a curator named Mr. McCandlish was
able to translate Runic sketches in Young’s

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:42.920
notes, identified as Tertiary Sumerian Cuneiform
circa 3400 BCE.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.720
Though they could not be fully translated,
the words “with loss” “Apakht” “ending”

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.300
“joy” and “permanence” were identified.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:57.010
In 2003, the SCP Foundation discovered the
item and brought it in for further study.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:02.790
The Lock is a small smooth, black, ellipsoidal
onyx stone with a white pattern that resembles

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.950
the cosmic microwave background, a pattern
of microwaves mapped by NASA that encompasses

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.900
the entire known universe.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.230
The stone is wrapped in complex layers of
gold filigree, which cover the stone’s center

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.410
as well as both the top and bottom.

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.500
At the top of the stone, the pattern of the
gold becomes too complex for the human eye

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.680
to grasp, and even defies resolution using
a microscope.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:29.170
This is thought to be the “keyhole”, as
it were, or the point at which The Lock could

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.060
become unlocked.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.230
The thing about locks is that they tend to
hold secrets.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.510
They almost always have something valuable
to hide.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:43.090
And anything that can be locked can be unlocked,
as long as you have the right key.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.480
The Foundation tried a variety of methods
to open The Lock, with no success.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.330
Ordinary lock-picking techniques were futile,
as were attempts to open it with a hammer,

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:56.740
chisel, sledgehammer, bolt-cutters, welding
torch, or bandsaw.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:02.310
Extreme heat, industrial cutting lasers, car
crushers, acid, plastic explosives, and even

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.730
an atomic warhead had no effect whatsoever.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:11.430
Dr. Hack terminated efforts to unlock The
Lock, also known as Project Pluto, on the

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.150
grounds that it was a fool’s errand, better
at wasting Foundation resources than producing

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:17.530
productive results.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:23.100
However, Dr. Mirski, who took over the research
team after Dr. Hack’s departure, resumed

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.160
the Project.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.280
They should have stopped trying to open it.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.480
Perhaps, if they had known what was waiting
to be released, what Apakht truly meant, they

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:32.510
would have…

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:37.780
In the lowest, most secure levels of Site
10, Dr. Yara Mirski held The Lock in her gloved

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.220
hands, feeling its heat pulse through the
latex.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.570
She was inspecting its intricate gold filigree,
looking at the place where a key should fit,

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:49.180
and pondering their many attempts to unlock
it.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.780
Nothing had worked yet, and perhaps that was
for the best.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:57.180
Though her colleagues were uncomfortable with
the idea of something that could not be unlocked,

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.160
a code that refused to be cracked, she had
the distinct feeling that an item that couldn’t

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.600
be opened by even the blast of a nuclear weapon
might be hiding something even deadlier.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.110
But she didn’t have time to finish that
thought.

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:15.320
A horn blared, shattering her concentration
and alerting her to the coming disaster.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:20.420
It was a warning, a sign that something had
breached containment.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.740
Only it wasn’t something trying to break
out.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.540
It was something breaking in.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.850
Hurriedly, she sat down the lock and opened
a hatch in the floor, removing a harpoon gun,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.720
outfitted especially for taking on powerful
anomalous enemies.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.850
She knew that the Harbinger was coming.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:40.030
There was little else she knew about the thing,
but she was certain that it was here for The

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:44.230
Lock, here to open it and usher in whatever
would come after.

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:47.890
She loaded a bolt into the harpoon gun and
prepared for its arrival.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:53.910
Then, all at once, there it was: glowing with
brilliant white light, almost too bright to

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:54.910
look at.

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:59.100
It was shaped like a human being, but she
couldn’t see its face.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.140
She couldn’t look at it for too long without
her head aching.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:06.860
A hundred wings sprouted from its back like
a biblical angel, its light permeating the

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.050
once-dimly lit chamber.

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:13.950
She was a researcher, not trained for combat,
but there was no other choice when she was

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:17.770
all that stood between The Lock and this otherworldly
being.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:21.090
She closed her eyes tight, and she squeezed
the trigger.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:24.680
The bolt flew and plunged directly into the
being’s chest.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:26.070
The Harbinger watched her.

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:30.800
Though she could not see its eyes, she felt
its gaze boring into her as it reached up

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:36.080
with one hand, and broke the harpoon into
pieces like it was nothing.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.360
It tossed the pieces to the side, now useless.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.400
Not knowing what else to do, overcome with
awe and terror, Dr. Mirski collapsed onto

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.400
her knees.

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:46.710
“Cut that out.”

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.570
The Harbinger said, urging her back to her
feet.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.080
It promised that it would not kill her.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.830
As she stared, open-mouthed, it produced a
small, simple object: a key.

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.160
Dr. Mirski pleaded with the being, asking
it to reconsider, to think about what might

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.340
be waiting inside The Lock.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.680
If the Harbinger had a visible mouth, it would
have smiled.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.639
It told her that it knew exactly what was
inside.

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:12.770
It was Apakht, and it would be released.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:17.510
As she watched, helpless, the Harbinger pushed
the key into the lock and turned.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:22.580
There was a flash of light, unbearably bright
and quick, the feeling of everything changing

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.900
across the world all at once.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:29.720
Suddenly, the being before her was not a creature
of wings and light, but a person.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:35.470
A person she almost recognized…but couldn’t,
like the Harbinger had a hold on her mind,

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.790
and wouldn’t let her access the memory.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:38.790
“Sorry.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:42.980
Nothing personal,” It said, as its form
shifted back to that painful brilliance.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:44.419
“What did you do?”

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:45.440
Dr. Mirski asked.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.660
“What did you unlock?

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:47.660
What’s Apakht?”

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:51.419
The Harbinger answered simply: “It’s the
End.”

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:56.270
Soon after Dr. Mirski met the Harbinger and
watched it open The Lock, a man named Freddie

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:59.300
Jones was hunting snakes near Tampa, Florida.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.790
A one thousand dollar prize had been promised
to the man who killed the longest python,

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:08.210
and fifteen hundred dollars were guaranteed
to the man who killed more pythons than anyone

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.300
else.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.770
Just days before, Freddie had been filling
up his truck at the local gas-n-go when he

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:18.790
stopped to watch the longest python he had
ever seen in his life slither across the asphalt

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:21.120
and into the nearby swamp.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.810
That was a surefire way to win if he’d ever
seen one, and now he was making his way across

00:08:25.810 --> 00:08:30.210
the wet, springy ground of the swamp in search
of his prize.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:34.560
After two hours of tramping through the wilderness,
all he had to show for it was a few kingsnakes

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:36.279
and some black racers.

00:08:36.279 --> 00:08:41.070
No pythons yet, and certainly not the extra-long
beast he’d seen before.

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:46.380
He was getting close to giving up, hot, exhausted,
covered in bites from fire ants, when he saw

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:50.170
it: smooth scaly skin, winding through the
underbrush.

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:52.550
He’d know that pattern anywhere.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:57.030
He snapped back into hunter mode, trailing
the creature as fast as he could.

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:01.210
His heart thumped against his chest and he
reminded himself his cardiologist said to

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:05.500
avoid strenuous exercise, but he’d already
come this far.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:06.880
No giving up now.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:09.410
His father hadn’t raised a quitter.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:14.510
Above him, a storm was brewing, thick clouds
gathering as thunder shook the ground and

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.220
lightning flashed uncomfortably close.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.100
He had to move fast.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.670
There was no doubt about it, his time out
there was limited.

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:26.230
He was right about this, but not for the reason
he thought.

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:30.200
He stumbled as the ground quaked, trying to
keep on his feet.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:34.450
The python was still in his eyeline, looking
longer and bigger as he got closer.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:37.990
He weaved through the tall grass, looking
for the snake’s head.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.560
He took six steps, then six more.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:42.560
There was no sign of its head yet.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.700
This thing was even bigger than he’d thought!

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:47.190
No way anyone else was taking home that cash.

00:09:47.190 --> 00:09:51.930
He felt his stomach drop as he realized that
he still couldn’t find the snake’s head.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:56.290
Its body stretched on and on, weaving all
around the brush.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:59.940
It was everywhere, and it was somehow…growing.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:04.810
He pointed his rifle at one side, where the
snake’s body had grown to the size of a

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:05.810
horse.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:10.529
That was impossible, he had to be seeing things,
but there it was.

00:10:10.529 --> 00:10:14.290
He squeezed the trigger and fired, the sound
lost in the thunder.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.350
The snake didn’t even react.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:21.870
It grew larger and thicker, winding around
him as he turned in circles trying to track

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:22.870
it.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:25.830
He didn’t even see the enormous head rising
over him.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.870
At least, until it blocked out the sun.

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:33.270
He opened his mouth to scream, but was crushed
beneath the body of the massive snake as it

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:37.640
slithered through the swamp, crushing plants
and animals as it went.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:42.230
Freddie Jones was not the only person to see
something strange at that moment, and he would

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:43.850
not be the last.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:48.370
A change was coming, and it was coming faster
than anyone could keep up with.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:53.170
In South Dakota, the king of the bears clawed
his way up from the earth with paws the size

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:54.170
of trucks.

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:58.410
A massive beast from the deep crushed a tour
boat off the shores of New England.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:03.000
A buffalo the size of a mountain shook itself
awake on the Great Plains.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:08.779
Across the world, from above and below and
beyond it, something was coming.

00:11:08.779 --> 00:11:12.150
An unknowable number of things, in fact.

00:11:12.150 --> 00:11:16.920
Entities from beyond the stars, from the ocean
floor, from the earth’s crust and mouse

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.160
holes in provincial homes.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:24.300
There were some that loved humanity, some
that hated it, some that could barely remember

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.080
what it was.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:32.200
Another was from the SCP Foundation itself,
someone who was not supposed to exist.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:33.710
None of them were, really.

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:38.550
But they all did, and they were all coming
to see what would happen next.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:43.340
This story is only just beginning…because
it wasn’t just one apocalypse that was unleashed

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.100
with the opening of The Lock.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:47.500
It was all of them.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:54.240
Now go check out “SCP-001 - When Day Breaks”
and “SCP-001 - The Scarlet King” for more

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:57.190
apocalyptic accounts of SCP-001!

